1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:01,872
Wednesday, eleven at night.

2
00:00:01,922 --> 00:00:05,160
Aziz is watching a football match when his phone buzzes.

3
00:00:05,210 --> 00:00:12,144
The mobile bank shows a login request from a phone he has never owned, at an address he does not know, one minute ago.

4
00:00:12,194 --> 00:00:15,192
Under it: Approve, and This was not me.

5
00:00:15,242 --> 00:00:17,064
Aziz taps This was not me.

6
00:00:17,114 --> 00:00:18,936
One tap.

7
00:00:18,986 --> 00:00:21,936
What must the platform do now, and what must it not do?

8
00:00:21,986 --> 00:00:28,344
In Lesson 30, the stranger with Aziz's SIM card tried a reset, and MyID stopped him.

9
00:00:28,394 --> 00:00:34,464
This lesson follows his other try, a normal login with a guessed password, and the button that answers it.

10
00:00:34,514 --> 00:00:37,320
First, how the request reached Aziz.

11
00:00:37,370 --> 00:00:41,592
The stranger typed Aziz's number and the correct password on a new phone.

12
00:00:41,642 --> 00:00:45,432
Only after a correct password does a new-device request start.

13
00:00:45,482 --> 00:00:50,496
TAF sends the code to the number, so it lands on the stranger's SIM card.

14
00:00:50,546 --> 00:00:55,128
At the same time, the platform finds Aziz's session in the mobile bank.

15
00:00:55,178 --> 00:00:58,488
It has rank two, so it gets the approval request.

16
00:00:58,538 --> 00:01:02,232
The push carries no code, only the request identifier.

17
00:01:02,282 --> 00:01:05,616
The new phone needs both: the code and the approval.

18
00:01:05,666 --> 00:01:07,488
Now Aziz refuses.

19
00:01:07,538 --> 00:01:09,744
Step one is the obvious one.

20
00:01:09,794 --> 00:01:16,392
The login on the new phone is refused, the attempt ends, and an event goes to the bank's antifraud system.

21
00:01:16,442 --> 00:01:18,576
But think about what this tap means.

22
00:01:18,626 --> 00:01:21,816
The request started only after a correct password.

23
00:01:21,866 --> 00:01:23,952
So someone knows Aziz's password.

24
00:01:24,002 --> 00:01:28,416
And that someone may also hold his SIM card, because the code went to that number.

25
00:01:28,466 --> 00:01:34,464
Every new code and every SMS alert to that number may land in a stranger's hand.

26
00:01:34,514 --> 00:01:37,152
So step two: the number goes on hold.

27
00:01:37,202 --> 00:01:44,016
While it is on hold, the platform starts no login that needs a one-time code to this number, on any phone or browser.

28
00:01:44,066 --> 00:01:47,040
It sends no SMS to the number at all.

29
00:01:47,090 --> 00:01:49,224
Notifications go by push only.

30
00:01:49,274 --> 00:01:55,896
So the marketplace website cannot help the stranger either: a web login that needs a code is refused.

31
00:01:55,946 --> 00:02:00,840
And Aziz's own sessions keep working, because their access does not need the number.

32
00:02:00,890 --> 00:02:03,528
Step three happens on the phone that refused.

33
00:02:03,578 --> 00:02:07,440
The mobile bank asks Aziz to change his password, at once.

34
00:02:07,490 --> 00:02:10,728
He types the old one, then a new one, two times.

35
00:02:10,778 --> 00:02:15,984
As after each password change, the other sessions of the same or a lower rank end.

36
00:02:16,034 --> 00:02:22,512
Aziz's session has rank two, so every other session ends, including any that the stranger opened.

37
00:02:22,562 --> 00:02:24,600
Step four is one question.

38
00:02:24,650 --> 00:02:26,280
Does your SIM card still work?

39
00:02:26,330 --> 00:02:29,832
If Aziz says yes, the stranger had only the password.

40
00:02:29,882 --> 00:02:33,120
The new password fixes that, and the hold ends.

41
00:02:33,170 --> 00:02:36,384
If Aziz says no, the stranger has his SIM card.

42
00:02:36,434 --> 00:02:38,928
Then the number must leave the account.

43
00:02:38,978 --> 00:02:43,896
Aziz was at level two, so first he passes a MyID re-check, on this phone.

44
00:02:43,946 --> 00:02:50,880
Then the platform removes the number, the same way as a number reassignment, and Aziz adds a new number.

45
00:02:50,930 --> 00:02:53,328
His account is not blocked at any point.

46
00:02:53,378 --> 00:02:59,160
At each step, the platform writes an audit event and sends an event to the antifraud system.

47
00:02:59,210 --> 00:03:02,016
And it sends the notification, number on hold.

48
00:03:02,066 --> 00:03:04,920
Why a hold, and not a removal at once?

49
00:03:04,970 --> 00:03:07,032
I think the answer is the wrong tap.

50
00:03:07,082 --> 00:03:11,328
People tap the wrong button, at eleven at night, during a football match.

51
00:03:11,378 --> 00:03:17,352
If This was not me removed the number at once, one wrong tap would take away Aziz's only login.

52
00:03:17,402 --> 00:03:20,088
The hold is safe and easy to undo.

53
00:03:20,138 --> 00:03:22,320
The SIM question decides the rest.

54
00:03:22,370 --> 00:03:24,312
The design pays a price for this.

55
00:03:24,362 --> 00:03:27,648
SMS notifications stop while the number is on hold.

56
00:03:27,698 --> 00:03:32,568
And a customer who has no session on any device cannot end the hold alone.

57
00:03:32,618 --> 00:03:35,088
A bank operator ends it, with a reason.

58
00:03:35,138 --> 00:03:36,288
One more detail.

59
00:03:36,338 --> 00:03:38,856
Only a rank-two session can refuse.

60
00:03:38,906 --> 00:03:45,360
A session of rank one, like a marketplace browser, gets a notification about the new login, but no buttons.

61
00:03:45,410 --> 00:03:50,112
Otherwise, a stranger with an old web session could approve his own login.

62
00:03:50,162 --> 00:03:53,304
To recap: This was not me refuses the login.

63
00:03:53,354 --> 00:03:58,176
Then the number goes on hold: no codes, no SMS, push only.

64
00:03:58,226 --> 00:04:01,800
The password changes at once, and the other sessions end.

65
00:04:01,850 --> 00:04:06,672
Then the SIM question: yes ends the hold, no removes the number after MyID.

66
00:04:06,722 --> 00:04:08,544
Aziz said no.

67
00:04:08,594 --> 00:04:13,536
His old number is now free, and one day the mobile operator gives it to someone else.

68
00:04:13,586 --> 00:04:16,944
When that person registers with it, what does the platform see?

69
00:04:16,994 --> 00:04:18,984
Next: when numbers change.
